WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:06.180
The lead actor of Candyman had a not-so-sweet
time on set, but he was richly rewarded for

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.220
his suffering.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.780
Tony Todd, who portrayed the terrifying ghost
of a man who was murdered in the late 1800s,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.110
revealed in an interview with The Guardian
that he earned a ton of extra cash while filming

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.060
the Bernard Rose-directed 1992 horror film.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:25.360
The star pocketed a cool $1,000 each time
he was stung by a bee during shooting, which

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.920
was a lot.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.720
In total, Todd took home $23,000 in addition
to his agreed-upon paycheck for the film,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.399
though he seemingly would have been fine without
the additional compensation, as he fully embraced

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:39.940
bees as an integral part of Candyman's story.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.360
The actor explained,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.479
"I negotiated a bonus of $1,000 for every
sting during the bee scene.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:49.049
And I got stung 23 times.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.600
Everything that's worth making has to involve
some sort of pain.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.299
Once I realized it was an important part of
who Candyman was, I embraced it.

00:00:56.299 --> 00:00:58.640
It was like putting on a beautiful coat."

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.520
Candyman features Todd's character often covered
in bees, but the most stunning moment happens

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:06.579
when a group of bees emerge from his mouth.

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:11.600
That sequence was achieved by placing actual
bees inside Todd's mouth, with a dental dam

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.320
in place to keep the critters from making
their way down his throat.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.829
The bees used in the film were actually specifically
bred to appear on the big screen.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:25.219
According to iHorror, the Candyman team used
newborn bees that were about 12 hours old,

00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:28.939
making them appear like adult bees with less
harmful stings.

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:33.289
Unfortunately, Candyman's lead actress, who
happens to be slightly allergic to bees, apparently

00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:37.549
went without a bonus, but she was still well
taken care of on set.

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:42.060
Virginia Madsen, who played graduate student
Helen in the flick, almost passed on the role

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.549
due to her allergy.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:47.990
When director Bernard Rose told her that she
probably was just psyching herself out, Madsen

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.570
got tested for insect allergies, and learned
that she was more allergic to wasps than bees.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:58.009
With that knowledge, Madsen had more confidence
heading into Candyman, and was comforted by

00:01:58.009 --> 00:01:59.929
all the precautions in place.

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:01.469
She once told HorrorNewsNetwork,

00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:06.259
"I really just had to go into this zen sort
of place and the takes were very short.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.119
What took the longest was getting the bees
off of us.

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:12.409
They had this tiny 'bee vacuum,' which wouldn't
harm the bees.

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:17.090
After the scene where the bees were all over
my face and my head, it took both Tony and

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.340
I 45 minutes just to get the bees off.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.110
That's when it became difficult to sit still.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.849
It was cool though, I felt like a total badass
doing it."

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:30.590
Now that everyone knows about Todd's bee sting
compensation deal with the original Candyman

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.090
team, the crew behind the upcoming remake
might start devising their own deal with the

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.090
actor.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:41.120
Get Out and Us director Jordan Peele has teamed
with Little Woods filmmaker Nia DaCosta for

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.760
a remake of Candyman, due out sometime in
2020, in which Todd is set to reprise his

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.830
iconic role.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.920
"Jordan has such respect for the horror that's
come before, I mean two of his favorite films

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.630
are Night of the Living Dead and Candyman,
so I got a small piece of both."

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:59.209
The OG star is stepping up to the plate for
the "reimagining," acting opposite Sorry to

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:04.930
Bother You actor LaKeith Stanfield and If
Beale Street Could Talk actress Teyonah Parris.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.150
The fact that Todd suffered dozens of stings
while filming the original means he'll probably

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.340
sustain just as many if not more during the
upcoming reimagining.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.010
Hopefully when Todd puts on that, quote, "beautiful
coat" to play Candyman again, the remake team

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.860
can also offer him stacks of cash for any
pain that comes with it.

